Pin the Metal shading-language version instead of inheriting it from the host.

Metal derives MSL version from the SDK the *host process* linked against when
MTLCompileOptions.languageVersion is unset. ggml is a library loaded into
whichever binary the user runs, so shader compilation depended on that binary
rather than on the machine. Under python.org's CPython 3.12 (linked against the
macOS 12.1 SDK) the embedded library fails to compile entirely --
"no matching constructor for initialization of 'threadgroup metal::half4x4[512]'"
-- and the Metal backend never initializes. Below MSL 3.1 the bf16 kernels are
additionally #undef'd by ggml-metal.metal while props.has_bfloat stays true.

Applied to all three ggml trees (ggml-*.patch): llama.cpp, whisper.cpp and
stable-diffusion.cpp each hit this through their own Metal backend.

The ladder deliberately stops below MSL 4.0, so the Metal 4 tensor headers stay
unavailable and the tensor probe keeps taking its existing unsupported path --
see proposed/llama.cpp-metal-tensor-msl4.patch for why cyllama does not want the
tensor kernels enabled.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-device.m b/ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-device.m
index 2dc6eb8..900bff1 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-device.m
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-device.m
@@ -104,6 +104,42 @@ int ggml_metal_pipeline_max_theads_per_threadgroup(struct ggml_metal_pipeline_wi
     NSLock * lock;
 };
 
+// When MTLCompileOptions.languageVersion is left unset, Metal derives the MSL
+// version from the SDK the *host process* was linked against -- not from the
+// running OS. That makes shader compilation depend on which binary loaded ggml.
+// Loaded into an interpreter linked against an old SDK (python.org's CPython
+// 3.12 links the macOS 12.1 SDK), the default drops below MSL 3.1 and this
+// library fails to compile outright:
+//
+//   error: no matching constructor for initialization of
+//          'threadgroup metal::half4x4[512]'
+//
+// leaving ggml_metal_device_init with no library and no Metal backend. Below
+// MSL 3.1 the bf16 kernels are also silently #undef'd (see ggml-metal.metal)
+// while props.has_bfloat stays true, so the failure mode there is worse than a
+// clean error.
+//
+// Pin the version so behaviour follows the machine, not the host binary. The
+// ladder stays below MSL 4.0: the Metal 4 tensor headers remain unavailable and
+// the tensor probe keeps taking its existing "unsupported" path.
+static void ggml_metal_set_language_version(MTLCompileOptions * options) {
+    // spelled numerically so this still builds against SDKs predating the enum
+    // constants, matching the MTLGPUFamilyMetal4_GGML precedent below
+    const NSUInteger msl_3_0 = (3 << 16) | 0;
+    const NSUInteger msl_3_1 = (3 << 16) | 1;
+    const NSUInteger msl_3_2 = (3 << 16) | 2;
+
+    if (@available(macOS 15.0, iOS 18.0, tvOS 18.0, *)) {
+        options.languageVersion = (MTLLanguageVersion) msl_3_2;
+    } else if (@available(macOS 14.0, iOS 17.0, tvOS 17.0, *)) {
+        options.languageVersion = (MTLLanguageVersion) msl_3_1;
+    } else if (@available(macOS 13.0, iOS 16.0, tvOS 16.0, *)) {
+        // MSL 3.1 does not exist here; 3.0 still compiles the library, but the
+        // bf16 kernels are compiled out -- an OS ceiling, not a choice.
+        options.languageVersion = (MTLLanguageVersion) msl_3_0;
+    }
+}
